WHAT: make KCalc a wk node -- default to the `wk` QPA platform, statically
      import the four plugins wasm cannot dlopen, link libqwk.a and the two
      component-type objects, compile a font in as a Qt resource -- and DROP
      KF6::Crash.
WHY:  four things a wasm32-wasip2 Qt/KF node needs that a desktop build gets
      for free, plus one framework that cannot come along.
        * QT_QPA_PLATFORM. plugins/qt qtbase was configured
          QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen (it predates the wk QPA plugin), so
          an unmodified app renders to nowhere and never opens a surface. Set
          only when the environment does not already say, so
          QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen stays available for bisecting failures.
        * Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN. No dlopen on wasm. Without QWkIntegrationPlugin,
          QApplication aborts with "no Qt platform plugin could be
          initialized" and an EMPTY plugin list -- it reads like a broken Qt,
          but it is an unreferenced symbol. QSvgPlugin/QSvgIconPlugin are what
          let SVG icons decode at all (KIconThemes REQUIRES Qt6::Svg and icon
          themes are SVG). KIconEnginePlugin is KIconThemes own icon engine,
          made STATIC by patches/kiconthemes-0001 -- it is what makes
          QIcon::fromTheme() give KDE theme semantics rather than Qt simpler
          lookup.
        * the component-type objects must arrive as LINK OPTIONS. They hold
          only a `component-type` custom section that wasm-component-ld reads
          to build the import list; nothing references them, so as archive
          members they are dropped and the binary ends up with no wasi:surface
          import.
        * fonts. Qt 6 ships none and a wk node has no host font dir; with none
          the app runs, QFontDatabase is empty, and every string renders as
          nothing at all.

      *** KF6::Crash IS REMOVED, AND THAT IS THE LOUDEST THING IN THIS PORT ***
      KCrash implementation is sigaction()/sigemptyset()/SA_RESTART signal
      handlers that, on a crash, fork() a child, setgroups()/setgid()/setuid()
      it, exec() drkonqi, waitpid() for it, and alarm() a watchdog. Every one
      of those symbols is absent from wasi-libc -- 15 distinct undefined
      identifiers in kcrash.cpp alone (fork, waitpid, kill, alarm, setuid,
      setgid, getuid, getgid, setgroups, sigaction, sigemptyset, sigaddset,
      SA_RESTART, SIG_UNBLOCK, RLIMIT_NOFILE) plus <QUnhandledException> in
      exception.cpp (FEATURE_thread=OFF). wasip2 has no asynchronous signals
      and no fork/exec at all.
      Building a KCrash that COMPILED would have meant gutting kcrash.cpp
      until KCrash::initialize() did nothing -- a stub wearing a framework
      name, which is a worse lie than an honest absence. So KCalc here has NO
      crash handler. When a wk node traps, wasmtime reports it to the host and
      the node dies; that is wk crash story, and drkonqi was never going to
      be part of it. This is the ONE framework of the fifteen that did not
      make it; see PORTING.md.

      All the wk-node changes are guarded (`#ifdef __wasi__`, `if(WK_...)`) so
      a desktop build of this tree is unaffected -- except for the KCrash
      removal, which is unconditional because CMake cannot easily make a
      find_package COMPONENT conditional on the target platform.
UPSTREAM: the wk-node half is as-is in spirit -- it is the standard static-Qt
      deployment shape. The KCrash removal is `no`: it is ours, it is
      wk-specific, and upstream would rightly want KCrash itself to grow a
      no-op platform backend instead.
